## 1. Introduction
Over the past decade, the selective formation of carbon-carbon (C-C) bonds [1] in organic synthesis can be successfully applied in diverse areas related with agrochemicals, high-tech materials and polymers, with a direct effect on our daily life. To date, Pd nanoparticles have shown excellent catalytic performance in many organic transformation reactions [23]. Therefore, novel Pd nanoparticles catalyst between organoboron nucleophiles with aryl halides have been developed for Suzuki-Miyaura cross-coupling reaction in recent decades. However, the nonspatial confinement effect, agglomeration of Pd nanoparticles of catalyst and harsh reaction conditions have become the urgent problems to be solved in Suzuki-Miyaura cross-coupling reaction. Therefore, to date, it is an extremely challenge to explore a catalyst with both the preeminent spatial confinement effect and synergistic promotion of Pd nanoparticles activity under the mild reaction.
The preeminent spatial confinement effect of catalyst can be assumed as best happening along with a pathway of effective collision of reactants to contact the catalytic active sites and the separation of the product by suitable pore structure as soon as possible [4,5]. Furthermore, it is very important to develop novel composites with anchored and dispersed active sites because the active sites tending to agglomerate. Moreover, the new composites can provide electron transfer to promote the formation of intermediate transition states of active components.
Nowadays, since new composites have the inherent advantages of high stability, large porosity, good synergistic properties and new shape [6–8], which have attracted considerable attentions for their myriad applications [9–11], including catalysis, separation, biomedical utilizations, gas capture and sensing, and so on. So far, a series of porous materials are combined into novel composites with different pore sizes in favor of catalytic selectivity to diverse reaction molecules. Unfortunately, precise control of Suzuki-Miyaura coupling reaction of the desirable production using noble catalysis is still challenging. Generally speaking, it is an increasing muber of difficult to effectively limit the space collision probability and prevent reunions of the nanoparticles with this traditional synthetic method, which hampers their practical applications seriously. Therefore, the novel catalyst not only offer good spatial confinement effect, but also keep high dispersion of nanoparticles.
Clearly, the spatial confinement effect can be realized by adjusting the pore size of porous organic frameworks [12–16]. Generally speaking, porous organic frameworks have emerged as supramolecular organic frameworks, covalent organic frameworks (COFs), and metal organic frameworks (MOFs). Many intriguing MOFs were constructed using the bonding of metal with organic functional groups resulting owning some advantages such as porous structure, easy synthesis and crystallinity [17]. In recent years, MOFs have acquired unprecedented prosperity with unique potentials in different fields such as catalyst, energy and other fields [18–26]. However, this kind of materials are also along with some drawbacks [27] such as low stability and lack of chemical functionality. In addition, as a support, the pure MOFs lack the space restriction to the reactant molecules. Obviously, these drawbacks result that mass transfer and heat transfer of catalytic reaction
can not be proceeded quickly, which is disadvantageous to effective molecular collision. These shortcomings have mainly restrained practical applications of MOFs in catalysis.
Developing a facile synthetic strategy for exploiting proper novel composites that make sure suitable pore size by growing shell to overcome above-mentioned problem. In particular, novel composites refers to MOFs using interfacing two porous materials or into another one porous material such as enzymes [28], graphene [29], metal nanoparticles [30] and COFs [31], thus enhance catalytic performance. From an applied perspective, the catalytic selectivity was improved by structural optimization [32,33]. Within the wide variety of materials, covalent organic frameworks (COFs), an environment-friendly material due to it is composed of O, H, N and C elements with low toxicity, are ingeniously constructed using strong covalent bonds across the principles of reticular chemistry [34–37]. Importantly, compared with the traditional mesoporous materials, the morphology, structure and pore size of COFs can be designed intelligently according to the structure–activity relationship. In a very interesting research, Li et al. [38] discovered 2D imine based COFs that showed excellent solvent adaptability. Besides, the well porous structure provides a confined porous environment and efficiently prevents NPs aggregation, increasing NPs stability. Applicability, researchers have demonstrated the superior potential of employing COFs materials in catalysis [39–42]. For example, Tan et al. [43] recently developed \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}@\mathrm{TpBD}\) hybrid materials. The resulting exhibited good photothermal conversion capabilities. Over the past decade, some researchers have tried to use Pd@COFs as a catalyst for Suzuki-Miyaura coupling reaction [44,45]. However, individual Pd@COFs still has some shortcomings in terms of stability and efficient anchoring of nanoparticles. In addition, many Pd@COFs catalysed Suzuki-Miyaura coupling reaction in organic media, which does not meet resource conservation or environmental protection requirements. So, it will be the future trend to catalyze the Suzuki-Miyaura coupling reaction in the aqueous medium. Moreover, pure COFs lacks a bridge to realize coordinate electron transfer of nanoparticles to form intermediate states. On this basis, MOFs and COFs can be constructed into MOFs@COFs. Additionally, some researchers have developed MOFs@COFs composites that exhibit remarkable advantages in different fields such as biomedicine, energy materials and catalytic materials, etc. For example, Fu et al. [46] reported [COF-300]/[ZIF-8] composite membrane and it demonstrate high separation performance of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{CO}_{2}\). Zhou et al. [47] synthesized Ce-MOF@COF hybrid nanostructure that have highly bioaffinity performances and electrochemical activity. However, as far as we know, the first combination will be the anchoring of NPs to the amino group of MOFs@COFs. Importantly, the composition of the iron salt plays a role in the activity of the resulting nanoparticles, which is attributed to the charge transfer of iron salt. This finding provides the basis for using Fe based MOFs (NH2-MIL-101(Fe)) as the core. To date, \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-MIL-101(Fe) @Pd@COFs has not been applied to the Suzuki-Miyaura coupling reaction. In the meantime, the research on the match of the catalyst with controllable pore size of shell and Fe-based copromotion with amino-dispersed active components on the inner core. Therefore, the research of the Suzuki-Miyaura coupling reaction by structure activity relationship will be a breakthrough in the
aqueous medium. Inspired by this, we were intrigued to probe the unprecedented probability of utilizing MOFs@Pd@COFs in catalysis.
Herein, we present innovatively a novel scalable route for two kinds of porous \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-MIL-101(Fe)@COFs and the encapsulated Pd NPs in order to achieve the spatial confinement effect and highly dispersed active sites. Based on this strategy, the COFs shell containing COFs(3 + 2) was prepared by three-two ring reaction of tris(4-aminophenyl)amine as binding angle with terephthalic aldehyde as binding bridge, and COFs(3 + 3) by three-three ring reaction of tris(4-aminophenyl)amine as binding angle with benzene-1,3,5-tricarboxaldehyde as binding angle. Because \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-MIL-101(Fe)@Pd@COFs(3 + 3) has appropriate porous sizes to effective collision of molecules, therefore, we can propose the hypothesis that \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-MIL-101(Fe)@Pd@COFs(3 + 3) can achieve ideal reaction performance in Suzuki-Miyaura coupling reaction in the aqueous medium. Additionally, Pd nanoparticles are well dispersed within as-prepared \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-MIL-101(Fe)@COFs using anchoring of electronic holes and electrons. To give full play to the steric confinement effect of the catalyst, the Suzuki-Miyaura coupling reaction was carried out under mild reaction condition by adjusting the pore size of the catalyst. The core of MOFs mainly plays the following advantages: exerting iron-based synergistic catalysis, improving the structural stability of the catalyst, anchoring Pd nanoparticles, providing appropriate acidity and basicity. The outer core COFs have the following advantages: anchoring the nanoparticles in coordination with MOFs, preventing the nanoparticles from falling off or agglomerating, controlling the pore size by different ring-forming methods, and exerting the spatial confinement effect. In this paper, combining the experimental results with the theoretical analysis, the effect of the steric confinement effect, Fe-based electron transfer synergy and highly dispersed active sites is further discussed in the aqueous medium. Therefore, this kind of new catalyst will become a future research topic in the field of catalysis.
## 2. Experimental section
## 2.1. Materials and reagents
\(\mathrm{FeCl}_{3}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) \((99.0\%)\), isopropanol \((97\%)\), N,N-dimethylformamide (DMF) \((99.5\%)\), 2-aminoterephthalic acid \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-DBC \((98\%)\), ethanol (EtOH) \((99.7\%)\), 1,3,5-benzene tetrachloroethylene \((97\%)\), terephthalaldehyde \((97\%)\), 3-amino-propyl triboxy silane (APTES) \((99.5\%)\), acetic acid (AcOH) \((97\%)\), tris(4-aminophenyl)amine \((97\%)\), 1,2-dichlorobenzene \((99\%)\), acetone \((97\%)\), dichloromethane (DCM) \((97\%)\), \(\mathrm{NaBH}_{4}\) \((97\%)\), palladium chloride \(\left(\mathrm{PdCl}_{2}\right)\) \((99\%)\), phenylboric acid \((97\%)\), iodobenzene \((97\%)\), 4-bromoanisole \((98\%)\), 4-bromotoluene \((99\%)\) and 3-bromobenzoic acid \((99\%)\) were purchased in Aladdin Industrial Corporation. Deionized water was used throughout all experiments. All chemicals (analytical grade) were used as received without further purification.
## 2.2. Catalyst preparation
\(\mathrm{FeCl}_{3}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) \((0.5406\ \mathrm{g})\), \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-DBC \((0.1812\ \mathrm{g})\) and DMF \((40\ \mathrm{mL})\) were added into \(100\ \mathrm{mL}\) three-necked flask, respectively. The solution was formed by stirring at room temperature. Then, the solution was transferred into a \(100\ \mathrm{mL}\) Teflon-line autoclave. After, solvothermal treatment was heated up \(110\ \mathrm{^{\circ}C}\) and maintained at this temperature for \(20\ \mathrm{h}\). The resultant brown precipitates were obtained after centrifugation. Next, in order to remove impurities and raw materials, the resultant brown precipitates was washed with EtOH and \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\). \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-MIL-101(Fe) was obtained at \(60\ \mathrm{^{\circ}C}\) for \(12\ \mathrm{h}\). \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-MIL-101(Fe) \((0.2\ \mathrm{g})\), APTES \((3\ \mathrm{mL})\) and isopropanol \((90\ \mathrm{mL})\) were added into \(250\ \mathrm{mL}\) three-necked flask. The solution was stirred by condensation at \(80\ \mathrm{^{\circ}C}\) for \(12\ \mathrm{h}\). The resultant brown precipitates were separated by centrifugation after the reaction was completed. Finally, \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-MIL-101(Fe) with amination was obtained at \(60\ \mathrm{^{\circ}C}\) and maintained at this temperature for \(8\ \mathrm{h}\).
was stirred by condensation at \(80^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(12~\mathrm{{h}}\) . The resultant brown precipitates were seperated by centrifugation after the reaction was completed. Finally, \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -MIL-101(Fe) with amination was obtained at \(60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) and maintained at this temperature for \(^{8\mathrm{~h~}}\) .
A certain mass of \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-MIL-101(Fe) with amination, 1,3,5-benzenetricarboxaldehyde \((8\ \mathrm{mg})\) or terephthalaldehyde \((6\ \mathrm{mg})\), 1,2-dichlorobenzene/EtOH \((1:1\ \mathrm{V/V}\ 6\ \mathrm{mL})\) were added into sealing tube. 3 M AcOH \((0.2\ \mathrm{mL})\) was dropout into the above solution after the centrifugation for \(20\ \mathrm{min}\). The synthetic reaction was thermally treated at \(80\ \mathrm{^{\circ}C}\) for \(24\ \mathrm{h}\) in \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) atmosphere. After that, the precipitate is then centrifuged and washed with EtOH. Two kinds of \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-MIL-101(Fe)@COFs(CHO) were obtained at \(60\ \mathrm{^{\circ}C}\) for \(8\ \mathrm{h}\) in vacuum.
The above obtained two kinds of \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-MIL-101(Fe)@COFs(CHO), 1,3,5-benzenetricarboxaldehyde \((8\ \mathrm{mg})\) or terephthalalcarboxaldehyde \((6\ \mathrm{mg})\), tris(4-aminophenyl)amine \((10\ \mathrm{mg})\) were added into sealing tube containing 1,2-dichlorobenzene/EtOH \((4:1\ \mathrm{V/V}\ 10\ \mathrm{mL})\) solution. 3 M AcOH \((0.2\ \mathrm{mL})\) was dropout into the above solution after centrifugation. The synthetic reaction was thermally treated at \(110\ \mathrm{^{\circ}C}\) for \(48\ \mathrm{h}\) in \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) atmosphere. The precipitate was then centrifuged and washed with EtOH, acetone and DCM. The resultant material was purified by Soxhlet extraction using dichloromethane. Finally, the \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-MIL-101(Fe)@COFs(3 + 2) and \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-MIL-101(Fe)@COFs(3 + 3) materials obtained by three-two ring reaction(3 + 2) and three-three ring reaction(3 + 3) for \(8\ \mathrm{h}\) under \(60\ \mathrm{^{\circ}C}\) vacuum, respectively.
\(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-MIL-101(Fe)@COFs \((100\ \mathrm{mg})\) and \(\mathrm{PdCl}_{2}\) \((10\ \mathrm{mg},\ 0.03\ \mathrm{mmol})\) were added in MeOH \((10\ \mathrm{mL})\). The mixture was stirred for \(24\ \mathrm{h}\) at room temperature, and then a solution of \(\mathrm{NaBH}_{4}\) in MeOH \((2\ \mathrm{M},\ 2\ \mathrm{mL})\) was added dropwise in to the mixture. The mixture was stirred continuously for \(2\ \mathrm{h}\) to obtain solids by Centrifugation, washed 3 times with MeOH and DCM, and then dried overnight at \(65\ \mathrm{^{\circ}C}\) to obtain \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-MIL-101(Fe)@Pd@COFs.
