## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1. Catalyst characterization
The synthetic schematic for the fabrication of \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -MIL-101(Fe) \(\mathcal{O}\mathrm{Pd}@\mathrm{COFs}(3\ +\ 3)\) and \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -MIL-101(Fe)@Pd@COFs \((3\ +\ 2)\) are accomplished following the strategy illustrated in Fig. 1. The pore structure, size, and functional group distribution of COFs \((3+2)\) and COFs \((3+3)\) are shown in Fig. S1. As depicted in Fig. S1, the porous size of \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -MIL-101(Fe) \(\mathcal{O}\mathrm{Pd}@\mathrm{COFs}(3+2)\) is larger than \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) - MIL-101(Fe) \(\mathcal{O}\mathrm{Pd}@\mathrm{COFs}(3+3)\) . Therefore, for Suzuki-Miyaura coupling reaction, \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -MIL-101(Fe) \(\mathcal{O}\mathrm{Pd}@\mathrm{COFs}(3+3)\) has high spatial confinement effect for the broad scope of the reaction materials in the aqueous medium.
\(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -MIL-101(Fe) \(\mathcal{O}\mathrm{Pd}@\mathrm{COFs}\) with different porous sizes were successfully prepared by a variety of reactions, where the compositions of \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -MIL-101(Fe) \(\mathcal{O}\mathrm{Pd}@\mathrm{COFs}(3+3)\) or \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -MIL-101(Fe) \(\mathcal{O}\mathrm{Pd}@\mathrm{COFs}(3+2)\) can be readily realized using adjusting the formation of COFs. Scanning electron microscopy (SEM), transmission electron microscopy (TEM) and X-ray diffraction (XRD) performed on \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -MIL-101(Fe) \(\mathcal{O}\mathrm{Pd}@\mathrm{COFs}\) revealed the successful encapsulation of \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -MIL-101(Fe) into COFs \((3+3)\) or COFs \((3+2)\) . A series of synthetic parameters about \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -MIL-101(Fe) and COFs were optimized. Catalytic activity was compared for Suzuki-Miyaura coupling reaction by changing the pore size of the COFs.
Fourier transform infrared (FT-IR) of \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -MIL-101(Fe) \(\mathcal{O}\mathrm{Pd}@\mathrm{COFs}(3\ +\ 2)\) and \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -MIL-101(Fe) \(\mathcal{O}\mathrm{Pd}@\mathrm{COFs}(3\ +\ 3)\) were compared (Fig. S2). There are four peaks at \(1378\ \ \mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) , \(1582~\,\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) , \(532~\,\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) , and \(758~\,\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) belong to the symmetric and asymmetric retractable vibration peaks of COO, bond peak of Fe-O, and the vibration peaks of the \(\mathsf{C{-}H}\) bond, respectively (Fig. S2(a)). This change can be verified that \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -MIL-101(Fe) has been formed.
\(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -MIL-101(Fe)@COFs \((3\ +\ 2)\) and \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -MIL-101(Fe)@COFs \((3\ +\ 3)\) shows \(\mathsf{C{=}O}\) bonds at \(1692~\,\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) and \(\scriptstyle-\mathsf{C}=\mathsf{N}\) bonds at \(1627~\,\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) , illustrating the formation of imine bonds between \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) and CHO. Specially, because there is no dipolar moment change in the imine bond, the new peak is relatively weak. Therefore, Raman spectroscopy will be used in order to verify the formation of imine bond. When Pd nanoparticles were loaded or not, the characteristic diffraction peaks on the whole framework did not
change obviously in Fig. 2(a). Therefore, the results reveal that \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -MIL-101(Fe)@COFs have good chemical stability.
There is a wide peak in the range of \(3200\,\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) to \(3600\,\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) for COFs in Fig. S2(b), which may be attributed to \(-\mathsf{N{-}H}\) peak. Therefore, it show \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -MIL-101(Fe)@COFs have been formed. As depicted in Fig. 2, Raman peaks can be observed between \(1500~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) and \(1700~\,\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) , which can be attributed to \(\mathsf{C{=}O}\) and \(\scriptstyle\mathsf{C}=\mathsf{N}\) peaks. Intensity order as follows: \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -MIL-101(Fe)@COFs \((3\ +\ 2)\ >\ \mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -MIL-101(Fe) \(\mathcal{O}\mathrm{Pd}@\mathrm{COFs}(3+2)\) and \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -MIL-101(Fe) \(\mathcal{O}\mathrm{Pd}@\mathrm{COFs}(3+3)\) \(>\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -MIL-101(Fe) \(\mathcal{O}\mathrm{Pd}@\mathrm{COFs}(3+3)\) . The order will be attributed to three-two ring reaction \((3+2)\) has more group vibration \(\scriptstyle\mathsf{C}=\mathsf{O}\) , \(\mathsf{C}{=}\mathsf{N}\) and \(\mathsf{C}{\mathrm{-}}\mathsf{C})\) than three-three ring reaction \((3+3)\) . In theory, 12 group vibration and 6 group vibration are formed in each pore of three-two ring reaction \((3+2)\) and threethree ring reaction \((3+3)\) , respectively. To our delight, Raman intensity is consistent with the theory. As mentioned above, the result is consistent with FT-IR spectra.
Element dispersion of \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -MIL-101(Fe) \(\mathcal{O}\mathrm{Pd}@\mathrm{COFs}(3+3)\) and \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -MIL-101(Fe) \(\mathcal{O}\mathrm{Pd}@\mathrm{COFs}(3\ +\ 2)\) were investigated by using EDS analysis. The elements C, Fe, O, N and Pd are dispersed in \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -MIL-101(Fe) \(\mathcal{O}\mathrm{Pd}@\mathrm{COFs}(3\mathrm{~\ensuremath~{~+~}~}3)\) and \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -MIL-101(Fe) \(\mathcal{O}\mathrm{Pd}@\mathrm{COFs}(3\ +\ 2)\) according to the formation of the associated valence bond \((\mathrm{Fe}{-}\mathrm{O},\mathrm{C}{=}\mathrm{N},\mathrm{C}{-}\mathrm{C})\) (Fig. 2(c) and Fig. S3). Pd elements in \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -MIL-101(Fe) \(\mathcal{O}\mathrm{Pd}@\mathrm{COFs}(3\ +\ 2)\) and \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -MIL-101(Fe) \(\mathcal{O}\mathrm{Pd}@\mathrm{COFs}(3+2)\) were detected at \(3\,\mathrm{keV}\) and \(3.5\;\mathrm{keV}\) , respectively. On the one hand, \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -MIL-101(Fe) \(\mathcal{O}\mathrm{Pd}@\mathrm{COFs}(3+3)\) and \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -MIL101(Fe) \(\mathcal{O}\mathrm{Pd}@\mathrm{COFs}(3+2)\) have exactly the same elements in their basic frameworks; on the other hand, \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -MIL-101(Fe) \(\mathcal{O}\mathrm{Pd}@\mathrm{COFs}(3+3)\) and \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -MIL-101(Fe) \(\mathcal{O}\mathrm{Pd}@\mathrm{COFs}(3+2)\) have different element ratios by using EDS analysis, which is consistent with results from Raman spectroscopy. This phenomenon may be due to the different ways of forming rings result in frameworks with different element ratios.
The successful fabrication of \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -MIL-101(Fe), \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -MIL-101 (Fe)@COFs and \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -MIL-101(Fe) \(\mathcal{O}\mathrm{Pd}@\mathrm{COFs}\) have been confirmed using SEM images. As shown in Fig. 3(a), \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -MIL-101(Fe) has the homogeneous octahedron structure with smooth surface. The homogeneous octahedron will can be improved the stability and kept the good morphology. As indicated in Fig. 3(b-e), ellipsoidal structures can be observed because \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -MIL-101(Fe) is wrapped a layer of COFs. Moreover, ellipsoidal structures of \(\mathrm{COFs}(3\ +\ 3)\)
and \(\mathsf{C O F s}(3\,+\,2)\) can also be obtained in Fig. 3(f) and Fig. 3(g). Therefore, the formation of catalyst can be verified by SEM images.
As shown in Fig. 4(a-b), the morphology of \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -MIL-101(Fe) as core and COFs as shell can also be clearly found for \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -MIL-101 \((\mathrm{Fe})@C O F s(3\ +\ 2)\) and \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -MIL-101(Fe) \(@\) COFs \((\mathbf{3}+\mathbf{3})\) in TEM images. As depicted in Fig. 4(c-d), the well-dispersed Pd nanoparticles of \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -MIL-101(Fe)@Pd@COFs \((\mathbf{3}+\mathbf{3})\) and \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -MIL-101(Fe) \(@\) Pd@COFs \((\mathbf{3}+\mathbf{2})\) can be observed in TEM. As shown in Fig. 4(c-f), the planar spacing of the atomic lattice fringes is \(0.22\;\mathrm{nm}\) , which is associated with the (1 1 1) plane of the Pd nanoparticles in \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) - MIL-101(Fe)@Pd@COFs \((\mathbf{3}+\mathbf{3})\) and \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -MIL-101(Fe)@Pd@COFs \((\mathbf{3}+\mathbf{2})\) . Therefore, it can be verified that Pd nanoparticles have similar size and dispersion inside \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -MIL-101(Fe)@Pd@COFs \((\mathbf{3}+\mathbf{3})\) and \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -MIL-101(Fe)@Pd@COFs \((\mathbf{3}+\mathbf{2})\) . In addition, the result can be further verified by using XRD and EDS analysis.
The as-synthesized \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -MIL-101(Fe)@Pd@COFs \((\mathbf{3}+\mathbf{3})\) and \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -MIL-101(Fe) \(@\) Pd@COFs \((\mathbf{3}+\mathbf{2})\) were characterized by X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) measurements to further reveal the incorporation of palladium within \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -MIL-101(Fe)@Pd@COFs \((\mathbf{3}+\mathbf{3})\) and \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -MIL-101(Fe) \(@\) Pd@COFs \((\mathbf{3}+\mathbf{2})\) . XPS has been built as an agile method to analysis the binding mode of the COFs with the correlating metal without the added isotopic labeling. XPS analysis confirmed the presence of Fe 2p, O 1 s, N 1 s, Pd 3d and C 1 s in \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -MIL-101(Fe)@Pd@COFs \((\mathbf{3}+\mathbf{3})\) and \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -MIL-101(Fe) \(@\) Pd@COFs \((\mathbf{3}+\mathbf{2})\) (Fig. 5(a-e), Fig. S4 and Fig. S5(a-f)), as expected in the framework structures.
The binding energy (BE) of the Fe 2p in \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -MIL-101(Fe) \(@\) Pd@COFs \((\mathbf{3}+\mathbf{3})\) with two peaks at \(725.8~\mathrm{eV}\) and \(711.6~\mathrm{eV}\) corresponding to Fe \(2{\tt p}_{1/2}\) and Fe \(2{\tt p}_{3/2}\) , which can be assigned to FeO (OH) and \(\mathrm{Fe}(\mathrm{OH})0\) , respectively. Therefore, it is can further
confirmed that the valence state of iron is trivalent, which provides the basis for the later catalytic process to transfer electron synergetic active components to obtain high catalytic activity. O 1s spectra at \(532.1\;\mathrm{eV}\) can be attributed to \((\mathsf{C}_{6}\mathsf{H}_{5})\mathsf{C}(\mathsf{O})\mathsf{C l}\), further indicating that \(\mathsf{N H}_{2}\) -MIL-101(Fe) has been formed. The N 1s spectra have a broad peak at \(399.5\ \mathrm{eV}\), indicating the formation of \(\scriptstyle{C=N}\) bonds. Therefore, it also confirms the formation of the COFs. Besides, the peak value of C 1 s spectrum appeared at \(284.9\ \mathrm{eV}\), which confirmed the formation of \(\scriptstyle{C=N}\) . As previously observed, the XPS
experiment found that the Pd in \(\mathsf{N H}_{2}\) -MIL-101(Fe)@Pd@COFs \((\mathbf{3}+\mathbf{3})\) is in its zero-oxidation state as assigned by its characteristic binding energy values of \(335.7\ \mathrm{eV}\), corresponding to two different spin–orbit pairs: Pd \(3\mathrm{d}_{5/2}\) and Pd \(3\mathrm{d}_{3/2}\). The results suggest the reduction of the \(\mathrm{Pd}^{2+}\) ions to \(\mathrm{Pd}^{0}\). Additionally, there are similar the binding energy (BE) of the Fe 2p, O 1s, N 1s, C 1s and Pd 3d in \(\mathsf{N H}_{2}\) -MIL-101(Fe)@Pd@COFs(3 + 2). Therefore, it can also be verified that Pd nanoparticles in \(\mathsf{N H}_{2}\) -MIL-101(Fe)@Pd@COFs(3 + 2) have been formed.
The atomic% of \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -MIL-101(Fe)@Pd@COFs \((\mathbf{3}\ +\ \mathbf{3})\) and \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) - MIL-101(Fe)@Pd@COFs \((\mathbf{3}+\mathbf{2})\) were listed in Table S1. As shown in Table S1, \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -MIL-101(Fe)@Pd@COFs \((\mathbf{3}+\mathbf{3})\) and \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -MIL-101 (Fe)@Pd@COFs \((\mathbf{3}+\mathbf{2})\) has an atomic% approximation of \(1.08\%\) and \(1.09\%\) for Pd 3d, respectively. This phenomenon indicates that the loading capacity of Pd nanoparticles is similar. C 1s \((76.07\%)\) and N 1s \((3.29\%)\) were detected in \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -MIL-101(Fe)@Pd@COFs \((\mathbf{3}+\mathbf{3})\) , while C 1s \((78.2\%)\) and N 1s \((4.24\%)\) were detected in \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -MIL101(Fe)@Pd@COFs \((\mathbf{3}+\mathbf{2})\) . The results confirmed that \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -MIL101(Fe)@Pd@COFs \((\mathbf{3}+\mathbf{2})\) contained more \(\scriptstyle\mathsf{C}=\mathsf{N}\) bonds and C—C bonds than \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -MIL-101(Fe)@Pd@COFs \((\mathbf{3}+\mathbf{3})\) . This conclusion may provide further evidence for the porous units in the framework. Therefore, the atomic% in the catalyst is consistent with the theory and Raman results (Table S1).
The crystal structure of \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -MIL-101(Fe)@COFs and \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -MIL101(Fe)@Pd@COFs were revealed by powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) analysis with Cu Kα radiation. The two characteristic peaks at \(9.02^{\circ}\) and \(11.08^{\circ}\) could be found (Fig. S6). These observed peaks are associated with the (200) and (110) facets for \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -MIL-101 (Fe), \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -MIL-101(Fe)@COFs \((\mathbf{3}+\mathbf{3})\) and \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -MIL-101(Fe)@COFs \((\mathbf{3}+\mathbf{2})\) . \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -MIL-101(Fe)@COFs \((\mathbf{3}+\mathbf{3})\) and \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -MIL-101(Fe) @COFs \((\mathbf{3}+\mathbf{2})\) have one characteristic peak at \(2.68^{\circ}\) , which can be verified the crystallinity of the spherical COFs. \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -MIL-101(Fe) is comparison with \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -MIL-101(Fe)@COFs \((\mathbf{3}+\mathbf{3})\) and \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -MIL101(Fe)@COFs \((\mathbf{3}+\mathbf{2})\) , these two characteristic peaks become weaker due to COFs shell directly influence characteristic peaks of \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -MIL-101(Fe).
\(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -MIL-101(Fe)@COFs \((\mathbf{3}+\mathbf{3})\) and \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -MIL-101(Fe)@COFs \((\mathbf{3}+\mathbf{2})\) have two main Bragg diffraction peaks at \(39.7^{\circ}\) and \(45.9^{\circ}\) (Fig. 5f and Fig. 5g), which may be assigned to the (111) and (200) planes, respectively (JCPDS file no. 87–0639). It is obvious from the plot that the average Pd nanoparticles crystallite sizes correlating to (111) reflection is higher than that of the other planes such as (200) reflection. This shows the preponderant orientation of (111) facet in Pd nanoparticles [48]. The results are in good agreement with the atomic lattice fringes of TEM. Moreover, it is well known that the average Pd nanoparticles crystallite sizes about \(11\ \mathrm{nm}\) [49], which could be anchored in \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -MIL-101 (Fe)@COFs \((\mathbf{3}+\mathbf{3})\) and \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -MIL-101(Fe)@COFs \((\mathbf{3}+\mathbf{2})\) . Therefore, it can be concluded that \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -MIL-101(Fe)@Pd@COFs \((\mathbf{3}+\mathbf{3})\) and \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -MIL-101(Fe)@Pd@COFs \((\mathbf{3}+\mathbf{2})\) have been formed.
