The yield of the product was \( 39.8\% \). The reason can be attributed to the lack of COFs shell, the stability of Pd nanoparticles on \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-MIL-101(Fe) will be reduced, and the agglomeration and shedding of Pd nanoparticles will occur during the reaction process, which will lead to a significant decline in catalytic performance.
From the point of view of the reactant electron, the electron cloud density directly affects the reaction process, especially the formation of intermediate states. The higher the density of electron cloud, the easier the reaction. The electron cloud density is according to the order substituent of \( 1 > \mathrm{Br} > \mathrm{Cl} \) under these reaction conditions. Due to the ether bond as the function of activating the para-group, it can be considered that the ether bond more advantages than H atom. However, in comparison with entry 2 and entry 4, the yield of entry 2 in Suzuki–Miyaura coupling reaction is higher under delicate conditions. Therefore, it could be concluded that the superior reaction activity of entry 2 may be due to the high electron cloud density become major influence factor in the Suzuki-Miyaura coupling reaction.
From the point of view of atomic properties of reactants, steric hindrance effect refers to the steric hindrance caused by the proximity of some atoms or groups in molecules. The larger steric hindrance will result in the inefficient collision of reaction molecules, resulting in the reaction can not be carried out. Generally speaking, CHO substituent with larger steric potential resistance than H substituent. Large steric potential resistance will impede the reaction process such as entry 3 and entry 4. It is noteworthy that the yield of entry 3 in Suzuki–Miyaura coupling reaction is higher, and the transition states with the active components are easier to form under mild conditions. Therefore, the experimental phenomenon agree with the theory.
Up to now, some attractive Pd loaded novel catalysts for the 4-bromoanisole- phenylboronic acid Suzuki–Miyaura coupling reaction have been reported. As indicated in Table S2, only a handful of catalysts shown high catalytic activity for 4-bromoanisole-phenylboronic acid coupling, but at higher temperature or with extra organic solvents or with a prolonged reaction time. Therefore, \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-MIL-101(Fe)@Pd@COFs(3 + 3) may be an effective alternative to Pd NPs catalysts for the Suzuki–Miyaura coupling reaction.
The stability and reusability of \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-MIL-101(Fe)@Pd@COFs \((3+3)\) was then examined in the Suzuki-Miyaura coupling reaction between iodobenzene and phenylboric acid. As displayed in Fig. S10, the catalyst with the characteristic diffraction peak keep good stability after recycling. The catalyst could be recycled five times, and slightly reduced catalytic activity was obtained which indicating the excellent reusability of \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-MIL-101(Fe)@Pd@COFs \((3+3)\) and \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-MIL-101(Fe)@Pd@COFs(3 + 2) (Fig. S11). The phenomenon may be attributed to oxidation of Pd active sites during the five consecutive runs.
## 3.3. Possible catalytic reaction mechanism
The Suzuki-Miyaura coupling reaction was carried out using TEA as alkali substance, \(\mathrm{R}_{1}\)-Ph-X and \(\mathrm{R}_{2}\)-PhB(OH)2 as reaction raw materials in the presence of \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-MIL-101(Fe)@Pd@COFs \((3+3)\) or \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-MIL-101(Fe)@Pd@COFs(3 + 2) as catalysts. The pore size of the catalyst not only affects the rate of the reactants entering the catalyst, but also affects the space distance of the reactants. Under the appropriate pore size space constraint, the reactants are easier to gather around the active site, and then the catalytic reaction is carried out. The appropriate pore size ensures the smaller free path of molecules, which is more conducive to the effective collision of molecules. The larger pore size is not conducive to the effective collision of molecules, which directly confirms the reason for the excellent catalytic performance of \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-MIL-101(Fe)@Pd@COFs(3 + 3).
According to the above experimental results and analysis, three elementary steps of possible reaction mechanism [52] which involves the addition oxidative-desorption activation-reductive elimination sequence can be found in Fig. 6. In adsorption oxidative step, aryl halides \((\mathrm{R}_{1}-\mathrm{X})\) diffuse through the COFs shell and then arrive near Pd NPs. In desorption activation step, Palladium leaching results from the oxidation reaction between permeating \(\mathrm{R}_{1}-\mathrm{X}\) and internal Pd nanoparticles, which transforms Pd (0) into Pd (II), \(\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}\) of \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-MIL-101(Fe) change into \(\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}\) and co-promote the formation of \(\mathrm{R}_{1}\)-Pd-X (Complex 1). The Complex 1 would then desorb and transfer across the COFs shell, entering into the reaction solution. In reductive elimination step, the formation of diaryl palladium species diaryl Pd (II) (Complex 2) by reaction of arylboronic acid and Complex 1. In the final step, the mainly aim production \((\mathrm{R}_{1}-\mathrm{R}_{2})\) can be obtained through the reductive elimination of Pd in the Complex 2 [53]. On the one hand, Pd(II) can be reduced to Pd(0) [54], on the other hand, \(\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}\) was oxidized to \(\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}\). As expected, the reduction of Pd (II) to Pd (0) can occur on the Pd NPs [55] between MOFs and COFs due to the larger size of Complex 2 than the pore size of COFs. Complex 2 is difficult to reverse diffuse through the COFs shell. To get a better idea of the whole process of the Suzuki-Miyaura coupling reaction, take the Suzuki-Miyaura coupling reaction of 4-bromoanisole and phenylboronic acid as an example to further study the mechanism (Fig. 6). In addition, spatial distribution of Fe-Pd in \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-MIL-101(Fe)@Pd@COFs can be shown in Fig. 6. It can be found spatial distribution of Fe-Pd is favorable for electron transfer and promotes the catalytic reaction. Therefore, more effective collisions, smaller steric hindrance, higher electron cloud density and halogen activation on the site-specific groups are all favorable for the conversion of raw materials to the target products.
## 4. Conclusions
In this work, we proposed a promising approach to construct the novelty core shell \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-MIL-101(Fe)@Pd@COFs catalyst by encapsulating MOFs in COFs with different pore sizes. \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-MIL-101(Fe)@Pd@COFs(3 + 3) provide some obvious advantages such as high stability, outstanding morphology, Fe-based electron transfer synergy and high-disperse Pd nanoparticles relative to the constituent catalysts. Pd nanoparticles were controlled anchored in COFs shell by amino group. \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-MIL-101(Fe)@Pd@COFs(3 + 3) and \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-MIL-101(Fe)@Pd@COFs(3 + 2) were used as catalyst for Suzuki-Miyaura coupling reaction by the broad scope of the reaction materials and the prominent yields of the products in the aqueous medium. Specially, \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-MIL-101(Fe)@Pd@COFs(3 + 3) show the superior catalytic selectivity and excellent stability. Furthermore, the spatial confinement effect has been confirmed by using experimental results. The present findings open preponderant opportunities toward the pore size design of nanocatalysts with outstanding selectivity for wide applications.
